    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d402-a1">

                <pb facs="0724" n="654" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg654"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 627.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Circular addressed to the consular officers of the
                        United States in Mexico.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-05-24"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 24,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to request that you will, as far as
                    possible, inform citizens of the United States who propose to purchase real
                    estate within the Republic of Mexico that unless they del are it to be their
                    intention to conserve their nationality as citizens of the United States, they
                    become, under Article XXX, Section III, of the federal constitution, in the
                    opinion of the Government of Mexico, citizens of Mexico, and that to such
                    persons no “<hi rendition="#italic">carta de matricula</hi>” acknowledging their
                    foreign nationality, will be issued from the department for foreign affairs. To
                    avoid all question, the reservation of citizenship should, in my opinion, be
                    made in the act of purchase.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;C.</p>
                <closer><signed>P. H. MORGAN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
